  "worked_solution": "Recall that the intersection of a family of indexed sets consists of those elements that belong to *all* the sets:\n\n$$\n{\u22c2}_{i\\in I}{A}_{i}={x\\,:\\,x\\in {A}_{k}\\,\\,\\text{for all}\\,\\,k\\in I}\n$$\n\nLet's now write down one of our indexed sets. For example, if $i=\\frac{1}{2},$ we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1/2}={\\frac{1}{2}}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]={\\frac{1}{2}}\\times [0,1]={(\\frac{1}{2},k)\\,:\\,k\\in [0,1]}.\n$$\n\nThis represents a vertical line segment from the point $(\\frac{1}{2},0)$ to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ in the coordinate plane, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/2827c5b33e84aff775d8bcc7ca98492afb220dda3a52fec4166afaa4da187d66.png)\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}={i}\\times [0,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi i)]={(i,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi k))\\,:\\,k\\in [0,i]}\n$$\n\nis the vertical line segment from the point $(i,0)$ to the point $(i,\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\pi i)).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072804/assets/5a108a3c0cdf37a072d135674b6ffdb3894b8c5ba142604ea7a434b0f42245f5.png)\n\nThere are no points that lie in all sets. Therefore, the intersection of the indexed sets is $\u2205,$ the empty set.",
